{"duration_api_ms":91204,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"95448de5-6032-4e28-96a4-c8f40ec6b14e","total_cost_usd":0.70391875,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11029,"cache_read_input_tokens":2075,"output_tokens":9656,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4628},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11029,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":9656,"cache_read_input_tokens":2075,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11029,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11029},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-fable-5-1":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":9656,"cacheReadInputTokens":2075,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11029,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.70391875,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":4628,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5-1","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Both titles are identical; the base document's title is kept.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Title: identical to the base title, stated once.\"},{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"reason\":\"Spacecraft name and encounter count corrected; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"reason\":\"Typo fixed; next planet, spacecraft and duration corrected; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"reason\":\"Spacecraft, observation type and start date corrected; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Light level factor corrected; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"reason\":\"Time standard, year and swapped distance units corrected; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"reason\":\"Moon count, names, author, year, ring counts and tilt corrected; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"reason\":\"Wind speed units, ocean and depth figures corrected; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\",\"reason\":\"Moon names and size description corrected; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\",\"reason\":\"Travel duration corrected from century to decade; see additions.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"reason\":\"Challenger date, crew count and timing corrected; see additions.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 3\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"No Voyager 3 flew; Voyager 2 is the spacecraft that visited Uranus.\"},{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"the three planetary encounters\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Before Uranus, Voyager 2 had flown past only Jupiter and Saturn.\"},{\"statement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"a future encounter with Saturn\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Saturn was already passed in 1981; Neptune followed Uranus in 1989.\"},{\"statement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Voyager 1 never visited Uranus; NASA's account gives 5.5 hours of close study.\"},{\"statement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Voyager 2 began long-range observation in Nov. 1985, before the 1986 flyby.\"},{\"statement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"corrects\":\"five-hundred times less\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"At about 19 AU sunlight is roughly 1/370 of Earth's; NASA states 400 times.\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"corrects\":\"17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The flyby was on Jan. 24, 1986, timed in UT; Voyager 2 launched in 1977.\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"corrects\":\"50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The units were swapped: 50,640 miles equals about 81,500 kilometers.\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Voyager 2 found 10 moons during the flyby; these are their official names.\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The names are Shakespearean; the first Uranian moons were found in 1787.\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Nine rings were known from 1977 occultations; Voyager 2 added two.\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"tilted at 66 degrees\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Uranus' magnetic dipole is tilted about 59 degrees from its rotation axis.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"corrects\":\"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The figure is 450 mph, which converts to 724 km/h; source units are garbled.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"corrects\":\"a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"NASA describes an ocean 800 km down; 800 km is 497 miles, not 479.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\",\"corrects\":\"Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus’ smaller moons\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Ariel, Umbriel and Titania are Uranus' moons; these five are its largest.\"},{\"statement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\",\"corrects\":\"nearly century-long travels\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Voyager 2 launched in 1977, under nine years before the 1986 flyby.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"corrects\":\"the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Challenger was lost Jan. 28, 1986 with seven crew, four days after the flyby.\"}]}","ttft_ms":50358,"type":"result","duration_ms":91268,"uuid":"9e5ab729-e963-461a-9254-ee0e34d85e18","ttft_stream_ms":2298,"time_to_request_ms":63,"first_content_frame_ms":2298,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
